WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.975 --> 00:00:10.510
My name is Taraz Lee.

00:00:10.510 --> 00:00:13.880
I'm an assistant professor in the
Cognition and Cognitive Neuroscience area

00:00:14.214 --> 00:00:17.217
in the Department of Psychology
at the University of Michigan.

00:00:19.652 --> 00:00:20.487
choking under pressure

00:00:20.487 --> 00:00:24.391
and how we fail in these high pressure
situations where we want to do our best.

00:00:26.259 --> 00:00:28.895
Choking under pressure is defined

00:00:28.895 --> 00:00:32.332
as situations in which people perform
worse than you might otherwise expect.

00:00:32.499 --> 00:00:34.501
Specifically
because they want to do really well.

00:00:34.501 --> 00:00:37.504
And it's a high pressure situation.

00:00:39.072 --> 00:00:40.240
I'm a basketball junkie.

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:42.742
I love playing basketball,
watching basketball.

00:00:42.742 --> 00:00:45.111
I still play a couple times a week.

00:00:45.111 --> 00:00:46.346
Even though I'm in my 40s now.

00:00:46.346 --> 00:00:48.548
My body's not happy, but I enjoy it.

00:00:48.548 --> 00:00:52.052
And I think I was always just curious
at what's going on in situations

00:00:52.052 --> 00:00:53.753
where people are really failing
under pressure.

00:00:53.753 --> 00:00:55.755
I'm sure anybody who's performed in

00:00:55.755 --> 00:00:58.758
anything has felt that pressure knows
they're not quite at their best.

00:00:59.259 --> 00:01:02.462
And as I learn more about, 
cognitive psychology and cognitive science

00:01:02.462 --> 00:01:06.933
and neuroscience, I started to wonder
what could be driving this failure.

00:01:07.367 --> 00:01:10.370
In these particular situations where
you really want to do your very best.

00:01:10.470 --> 00:01:15.542
When you play basketball, do you try
and put yourself in a certain mindset?

00:01:15.809 --> 00:01:16.843
I never choke under pressure.

00:01:23.483 --> 00:01:26.820
So in a lot of, experiments
looking at choking under pressure,

00:01:26.853 --> 00:01:30.190
you have participants come into your lab,
do some sort of task, are interested

00:01:30.190 --> 00:01:34.928
in whether that be golf putting or a math
test and the manipulation that tends to,

00:01:35.028 --> 00:01:37.831
put people in a lot of stress
and pressure is you tell them

00:01:37.831 --> 00:01:41.568
that they're playing for cash bonuses,
that they're paired with the partner.

00:01:41.768 --> 00:01:42.902
Their partner did great.

00:01:42.902 --> 00:01:45.672
They're only going to get paid out
if both of you do well.

00:01:45.672 --> 00:01:47.006
Now it's your turn,

00:01:47.006 --> 00:01:48.775
and people have a real hard time
with that situation,

00:01:48.775 --> 00:01:51.311
and tend to do a lot worse
than you might otherwise expect.

00:01:51.311 --> 00:01:53.446
The addition of the partner, I think, is

00:01:53.446 --> 00:01:56.449
really the kills playing for

00:01:56.616 --> 00:02:00.320
that social pressure of playing
for a buddy who's just did great.

00:02:00.353 --> 00:02:01.454
Now it's all riding on you.

00:02:01.454 --> 00:02:03.323
People do really poorly in that situation.

00:02:05.558 --> 00:02:07.927
Typically there's

00:02:07.927 --> 00:02:09.295
two different kind of theories

00:02:09.295 --> 00:02:11.464
of what's going on
when people are choking under pressure.

00:02:11.464 --> 00:02:14.234
One which you might expect
is that when you're in a high pressure

00:02:14.234 --> 00:02:16.269
situation, you get really distracted.

00:02:16.269 --> 00:02:21.975
So you start focusing on worries
about failure failure failure or what

00:02:21.975 --> 00:02:25.778
people are going to think if you fail
instead of focusing on the task at hand.

00:02:26.412 --> 00:02:29.382
So there's a whole host of studies showing
that that something like that happens.

00:02:29.516 --> 00:02:32.452
If you happen to be an expert
in the particular thing that you're doing.

00:02:32.452 --> 00:02:36.222
So let's say you are a professional golfer
and it's a putt on the last day

00:02:36.222 --> 00:02:38.124
of a tournament or something like that.

00:02:38.124 --> 00:02:40.293
It also turns out that if you're an expert

00:02:40.293 --> 00:02:43.696
and you focus on what your body is doing,
especially if it's a movement skill,

00:02:43.696 --> 00:02:46.566
like if you're a musician or an athlete

00:02:46.566 --> 00:02:47.767
focusing on what your body is doing

00:02:47.767 --> 00:02:49.736
when you're an expert
has a whole host of problems.

00:02:49.736 --> 00:02:52.071
You're not good because you're thinking
about what your body is doing.

00:02:52.071 --> 00:02:54.474
So we all know about muscle memory.

00:02:54.474 --> 00:02:58.077
And it turns out there's different parts
of the brain that are really important

00:02:58.077 --> 00:03:01.181
for those expert skills,
that aren't the kinds of parts of brain

00:03:01.214 --> 00:03:02.315
they have conscious access to.

00:03:06.286 --> 00:03:08.388
depending on the skill, obviously, but

00:03:08.388 --> 00:03:11.357
there's an area brain called the striatum,
which is in the basal ganglia.

00:03:11.424 --> 00:03:12.292
So different areas,

00:03:12.292 --> 00:03:15.295
the basal ganglia, very important
for these more procedural AIS skills.

00:03:15.295 --> 00:03:19.132
I mean, it seems like
those areas of the brain that are really,

00:03:19.599 --> 00:03:22.302
helping to perform those high pressure
situations, they learn really slowly.

00:03:22.302 --> 00:03:24.537
But once you have learned
they're really resilient.

00:03:24.537 --> 00:03:28.675
And are there brain areas
associated with consciously

00:03:28.675 --> 00:03:31.678
trying hard to think about your body
when you're a novice?

00:03:32.278 --> 00:03:35.949
Yeah, it turns out early on in learning,
when you really have to focus

00:03:35.949 --> 00:03:37.684
and pay attention
to what you're doing an area of

00:03:37.684 --> 00:03:41.321
your brain called the prefrontal cortex,
which is right behind your forehead.

00:03:41.621 --> 00:03:45.892
It's very important for things
like paying attention, focusing,

00:03:46.226 --> 00:03:47.026
those days brain

00:03:47.026 --> 00:03:50.063
that seem to be more active
when you're just learning a new skill

00:03:50.063 --> 00:03:51.431
and when you're trying to
focus on what you're doing.

00:03:54.534 --> 00:03:55.235
Sure.

00:03:55.235 --> 00:03:59.906
My favorite anecdote about choking under
pressure comes from basketball.

00:03:59.939 --> 00:04:01.307
Like I said, I'm a basketball junkie.

00:04:01.307 --> 00:04:06.713
In the 1995 NBA finals, the Orlando
Magic, which was Shaquille O'Neal’s team.

00:04:06.713 --> 00:04:08.548
I think most people have heard
of Shaquille O'Neal.

00:04:08.615 --> 00:04:10.984
They're playing the Houston Rockets.

00:04:10.984 --> 00:04:13.987
It was the first game
of the finals of the NBA finals

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:17.523
the Orlando Magic were winning by three
with about 10s left.

00:04:18.758 --> 00:04:21.427
Any points would essentially steal
the game.

00:04:21.427 --> 00:04:24.063
A guard on the Orlando Magic name,
Nick Anderson.

00:04:24.230 --> 00:04:26.165
He got fouled with 10s left.

00:04:26.165 --> 00:04:29.068
When you get fouled in basketball, you get
two free throws, each with one point.

00:04:29.068 --> 00:04:30.303
He makes either one of those.

00:04:30.303 --> 00:04:31.871
They just win the game.

00:04:31.871 --> 00:04:33.172
Steps up the line.

00:04:33.172 --> 00:04:35.942
Takes his first shot. Misses.

00:04:35.942 --> 00:04:38.044
Seems to get a little nervous.
Gets a little tight.

00:04:38.044 --> 00:04:40.313
Second shot very short.

00:04:40.313 --> 00:04:43.316
Bounces right off the rim but miraculously
comes right back into his hands.

00:04:43.783 --> 00:04:45.952
He has another chance.
They foul him again.

00:04:45.952 --> 00:04:47.253
He gets two more free throws.

00:04:47.253 --> 00:04:50.256
Even one of those free throws ices
the game.

00:04:50.390 --> 00:04:52.625
He's a 70% free throw shooter.

00:04:52.625 --> 00:04:55.428
He proceeds to miss the next two free
throws as well.

00:04:55.428 --> 00:04:56.863
The rockets get the rebound.

00:04:56.863 --> 00:04:57.897
They hit a three pointer.

00:04:57.897 --> 00:04:59.866
The game goes to overtime.

00:04:59.866 --> 00:05:01.701
And the Orlando Magic
end up losing that game.

00:05:04.837 --> 00:05:07.573
And Nick Anderson's always remembered
as a player who’s choked under pressure.

00:05:13.546 --> 00:05:15.782
So we don't really know too much
about what separates

00:05:15.782 --> 00:05:19.118
the person that excels under pressure
and the person that chokes under pressure.

00:05:19.285 --> 00:05:21.387
A few things we do
know, though, are that,

00:05:21.387 --> 00:05:22.755
those people who we think
are really clutch

00:05:22.755 --> 00:05:25.558
and do awesome under pressure,
they're not actually doing better.

00:05:25.558 --> 00:05:28.761
They just don't do as poorly
as everybody else does, said they.

00:05:28.795 --> 00:05:31.497
Everybody
kind of does worse in this situations.

00:05:31.497 --> 00:05:34.500
But people who are really good,
they suffer less than other folks do.

00:05:34.734 --> 00:05:36.803
From a lot of the experimental research
that we've done,

00:05:36.803 --> 00:05:40.239
it seems like
there may be some things that can predict

00:05:40.239 --> 00:05:42.208
who is more likely to choke
in the pressure.

00:05:42.208 --> 00:05:45.345
Specifically, impulsive individuals
I found in my work

00:05:45.345 --> 00:05:47.080
tend to be the ones that

00:05:47.080 --> 00:05:49.849
choke on the pressure a little bit more
than your average individual.

00:05:49.849 --> 00:05:51.517
And there's also some other work
suggesting that

00:05:51.517 --> 00:05:54.554
if your brain loss averse it's called
if you're very scared of losing

00:05:54.554 --> 00:05:58.424
rather than being driven by the gains
and by winning,

00:05:58.424 --> 00:06:00.760
those individuals tend to do a little bit
worse under pressure as well.

00:06:06.999 --> 00:06:07.266
I think a

00:06:07.266 --> 00:06:11.237
common one that especially students
think about is just test anxiety.

00:06:11.270 --> 00:06:14.107
So test anxiety, math anxiety,
things like this.

00:06:14.107 --> 00:06:16.309
People really want to do
well on their exam,

00:06:16.309 --> 00:06:17.877
but they're really worried about failure

00:06:17.877 --> 00:06:19.712
and they tend to do really poorly
in that situation.

00:06:19.912 --> 00:06:22.882
Are there any tips for how to avoid that?

00:06:22.882 --> 00:06:25.618
We don't know a ton
about what can protect you against,

00:06:25.618 --> 00:06:28.721
choking under pressure
and these kinds of, anxious moments.

00:06:28.721 --> 00:06:32.492
The one thing that does seem to help
a little bit is if you externalize

00:06:32.492 --> 00:06:33.059
where you're thinking.

00:06:33.059 --> 00:06:36.062
So if you have a journal
or just jot down on a piece of paper

00:06:36.062 --> 00:06:38.564
what you're actually worried about,
that tends to get it out of your mind

00:06:38.564 --> 00:06:40.166
and lets you better
focus on the task at hand.

00:06:44.036 --> 00:06:44.270
Yeah.

00:06:44.270 --> 00:06:47.140
What was Pavlov's hair so soft? Why?

00:06:47.140 --> 00:06:48.141
Classical conditioning.

